<div class="wrap prose">
  <p class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/guides/">Guides</a> &rsaquo; Scale ruler</p>
  <h1>How to use a scale ruler</h1>
  <p class="lead muted">A scale ruler lets you read real-world dimensions straight off a drawing without doing any arithmetic &mdash; once you understand what the scales mean.</p>

  <p>A scale ruler (or scale rule) is a specialist ruler pre-divided so that a drawing at a given ratio can be measured directly in real dimensions. Instead of measuring millimetres on paper &amp; multiplying, you pick the matching scale &amp; read the true size off the ruler. It is one of the first tools you will buy for architecture &amp; one you will use constantly.</p>

  <h2>What the scales mean</h2>
  <p>A scale is written as a ratio such as 1:50. This means one unit on the drawing represents fifty of the same units in reality. So 1mm on paper is 50mm in life, &amp; 20mm on paper is 1,000mm (one metre). The larger the second number, the smaller &amp; more zoomed-out the drawing. A key point for beginners: 1:20 is a bigger, more detailed drawing than 1:200, even though 200 is the larger number.</p>
  <table class="data"><tr><th>Scale</th><th>Typical use</th></tr>
    <tr><td>1:1 &ndash; 1:5</td><td>Full-size &amp; large detail drawings</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1:10 &ndash; 1:20</td><td>Construction details, joinery, sections</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1:50</td><td>Floor plans &amp; sections of a single dwelling</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1:100</td><td>General arrangement plans of a building</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1:200 &ndash; 1:500</td><td>Larger buildings &amp; site plans</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1:1250 &ndash; 1:2500</td><td>Location plans &amp; mapping</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2>Reading a measurement</h2>
  <p>Find the edge of the ruler marked with the scale you need &mdash; each edge or face has its ratio printed at the zero end. Line the zero up with the start of the line you want to measure, then read directly along to the far end. The number you read is the real dimension, usually in metres or millimetres depending on how that scale face is labelled. There is no calculation: the ruler has done the maths for you.</p>

  <h2>Worked example at 1:50</h2>
  <p>You have a plan drawn at 1:50 &amp; want the length of a bedroom wall. Put the 1:50 zero at one corner &amp; read to the other. If the mark falls on 3.6, the wall is 3.6 metres (3,600mm) long. To check by hand: on paper that wall measures 72mm, &amp; 72 &times; 50 = 3,600mm. The ruler simply spares you that multiplication every time.</p>

  <h2>Worked example at 1:100</h2>
  <p>The same building is also printed at 1:100 on an A3 general arrangement sheet. Now that 3.6m wall measures just 36mm on paper. Read it on the 1:100 face &amp; you still get 3.6m &mdash; the drawing is half the size, but the correct scale face gives the true dimension. This is exactly why you must match the ruler face to the drawing scale, never eyeball it.</p>

  <h2>Triangular vs flat rulers</h2>
  <p>A triangular scale rule has three faces &amp; up to six scales, so it carries the most common ratios in one tool &amp; is very durable &mdash; but it is bulkier &amp; you have to rotate it to find the right face. A flat (oval) scale rule is slim, sits flat on the board &amp; is easy to slip in a bag, but carries fewer scales. Many students keep a flat rule for studio &amp; a triangular one at their desk.</p>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <table class="data"><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>Fix</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Reading the wrong face</td><td>Always confirm the printed ratio matches the drawing scale</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Scaling off a resized print</td><td>Only scale from a sheet printed at its true size &mdash; a shrunk PDF is worthless</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Confusing metres &amp; millimetres</td><td>Check whether the face reads whole metres or mm</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Not starting at zero</td><td>Align the zero mark, not the ruler end, with your line</td></tr>
  </table>

  <div class="callout warn"><strong>Never scale off a screen or a reduced printout.</strong> If a drawing has been shrunk to fit the paper, the scale is wrong &amp; every measurement you take will be too. Trust written dimensions over a scaled one whenever both exist.</div>
</div>
